+## Durations
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a string such as `1h30m` or `1m500ms` into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The
+string is one or more integer parts, each followed by one of the units `d`, `h`, `m`, `s`, or `ms`. Each unit may appear
+once, largest first, with no separators. A bare integer is a number of seconds, and negative durations are rejected.
+
+`min` and `max` restrict the accepted durations. Both bounds are inclusive, and either one may be omitted.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    import datetime
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(max=datetime.timedelta(hours=1)), default="90s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(timeout.total_seconds())
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1m500ms'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=2h'])
+```
+
